## Why loading secrets from a plain‑text `.env` file is problematic in production

| Risk | Description |
|------|-------------|
| **Accidental commit** – `.env` files often get added to source control, exposing real keys. |
| **Runtime access** – Any process (or attacker who gains shell access) can read the file directly. |
| **Rotation & audit** – Updating a secret means changing code/re‑deploying, and you lose a central audit trail. |
| **Environment leakage** – When the app is containerised, the file may be baked into images and end up in registries. |

Because of these issues, **real production secrets should never be stored in code‑base files**. They should be supplied at runtime via a secure secrets‑management solution.

