### 3. Input Validation & Sanitisation

Even though the current routes don’t accept parameters, any future route that does should validate the payload. A lightweight yet powerful option is **celebrate** (Joi wrapper).

```js
const { celebrate, Joi, Segments } = require('celebrate');

app.post('/login',
  celebrate({
    [Segments.BODY]: Joi.object({
      email: Joi.string().email().required(),
      password: Joi.string().min(8).max(128).required()
    })
  }),
  (req, res) => {
    // safe to trust req.body.email/password here
    const token = issueToken({ sub: req.body.email, roles: ['user'] });
    res.json({ token });
  }
);
```

### 4. Centralised Error Handling (Avoid leaking stack traces)

```js
// Place after all routes
app.use((err, req, res, next) => {
  // Log the full error for developers/monitoring
  console.error(err);

  // Return a generic message to the client
  const status = err.status || 500;
  res.status(status).json({
